  "subtitle": "The calculator that became a musical instrument — and vice versa",
  "why_scout_cares": "A 29-button keypad where every key wears two hats: digit/operator AND musical note. A mode switch toggles the entire keyboard between calculator and synthesizer, making this one of the purest examples of modal hardware design in consumer electronics. ADSR synthesis parameters are entered as 8-digit numeric codes — sound design through arithmetic. $69.95 in 1981, used by The Human League, Trio ('Da Da Da'), and a generation of bedroom musicians exploiting its lo-fi kitsch. Held by the Vienna Technical Museum. 3 CC-licensed photos available including a CC0 museum piece.",
